WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.140
In 1953 a surgeon removed tissue from the inner part of both temporal lobes of a

2
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.820
young man. He had severe epilepsy.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.360
Afterwards, the patient known as H.M.

4
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.400
could not remember events from an hour earlier.

5
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.600
Yet he could still learn a new hand skill.

6
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.900
Is memory one ability, or several?

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:28.903
The test is a dissociation.

8
00:00:29.823 --> 00:00:33.383
One ability fails after damage, while another survives.

9
00:00:34.523 --> 00:00:36.463
H.M.'s digit span was normal.

10
00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:39.983
That is how many digits he could repeat back at once.

11
00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:43.723
He could hold a number as long as he kept rehearsing it.

12
00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:45.503
So working memory worked.

13
00:00:46.143 --> 00:00:50.303
But nothing on that notepad reached long-term declarative memory.

14
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:52.643
His skill learning survived too.

15
00:00:53.517 --> 00:00:57.937
One group failing one task is a single dissociation.

16
00:00:58.817 --> 00:01:01.177
It could mean only that the task is harder.

17
00:01:02.197 --> 00:01:06.997
So take a worked problem, with invented numbers, in the design of the Knowlton

18
00:01:06.997 --> 00:01:09.417
study. There are two tasks.

19
00:01:09.977 --> 00:01:13.657
A two-choice classification, where chance is 50%.

20
00:01:13.657 --> 00:01:18.997
And a four-choice questionnaire about the training, where chance is 25%.

21
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.790
Controls score 70 and 80.

22
00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.370
Amnesic patients score 68 and 40.

23
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.090
Parkinson's patients score 52 and 78.

24
00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.730
Look at 40 against 80.

25
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.910
It looks like half of normal.

26
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.590
But raw scores hide the guessing.

27
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:40.933
So subtract chance first.

28
00:01:41.593 --> 00:01:44.973
Then divide by the controls' gain above chance.

29
00:01:45.953 --> 00:01:51.773
The amnesic questionnaire gives 15 over 55, which is 27%.

30
00:01:51.773 --> 00:01:57.593
That is about a quarter, not a half, because a quarter of answers are right by

31
00:01:57.593 --> 00:02:02.473
guessing. Amnesic classification gives 90%.

32
00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:06.793
Parkinson's patients get 10%, and 96%.

33
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:11.933
Now compare. Each group fails the task the other passes.

34
00:02:12.593 --> 00:02:14.273
That is a double dissociation.

35
00:02:15.193 --> 00:02:20.633
No single difficulty scale fits, because the task that is hard for one group is easy

36
00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:24.393
for the other. It shows two separable learning processes.

37
00:02:25.233 --> 00:02:30.353
It does not pin each to one structure, because Parkinson's disease damages more than

38
00:02:30.353 --> 00:02:36.093
the striatum. A lesion shows that a structure is needed, not that the memory lives there.
